<BaseLayout title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    Practical, source-aware marketing ideas for the people growing residential
    real estate businesses.
  </p>

  <h2>Who this blog is for</h2>
  <p>
    This blog is for freelance marketers who work with residential real estate
    agents and brokerages in the United States. If you help clients with SEO,
    content marketing, or visibility in AI-powered search, you’ll find practical
    ideas to adapt to your work.
  </p>
  <p>
    It’s also for independent agents and smaller brokerages that don’t have a
    large marketing team or budget. The goal is to help you learn useful skills,
    apply them yourself when it makes sense, and make informed decisions about
    when specialist support could help.
  </p>
  <p>
    The guidance here is meant to help you work more effectively and build
    stronger services or businesses. Results depend on the market, the work, and
    many factors outside any one marketing workflow.
  </p>

  <h2>What we cover</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Local SEO and neighborhood-level content for residential real estate</li>
    <li>Generative Engine Optimization (GEO) and visibility in AI-powered search</li>
    <li>Content-planning playbooks for real estate marketing work</li>
    <li>Practical AI workflows and automation ideas</li>
    <li>Ways to evaluate evidence, sources, and performance</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we use AI</h2>
  <p>
    Some articles begin with an AI research agent that searches the web and
    extracts information from pages it finds. AI can help organize research and
    prepare drafts, but it is not treated as a source of truth. The original
    pages are the sources.
  </p>
  <p>
    Search results can suggest where to investigate; they do not, by themselves,
    prove how many people search for a topic or what buyers in a specific area
    want. We label suggestions carefully and avoid presenting them as confirmed
    demand without supporting evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>How we check information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Research reports should include the source URLs used.</li>
    <li>We check whether each source supports the specific claim, place, and time period.</li>
    <li>If evidence is limited, we narrow or qualify the claim, or leave it out rather than fill the gap with a guess.</li>
    <li>We link to original sources where readers can review them.</li>
    <li>We distinguish sourced facts from AI-generated ideas and editorial recommendations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you spot an error or a source that does not support a claim, let us know
    so we can review and correct it.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
